WEBVTT

00:33:28.304 --> 00:33:30.766
the research points to
a few effective strategies.

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:33.376
One is looking for uncommon commonalities,

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:35.866
similarities you share that are rare.

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:38.676
A second is asking
those open-ended questions,

00:33:38.700 --> 00:33:41.366
showing real curiosity
about the other person --

00:33:41.390 --> 00:33:43.415
as long as you don't put them on the spot

00:33:43.439 --> 00:33:44.996
or cross any lines.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:46.796
A third is self-disclosure,

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:48.886
sharing something about yourself.

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.701
That's what Jessica was responding to

00:33:50.725 --> 00:33:53.277
when Bess opened up
about her sad desk lunch.

00:33:53.934 --> 00:33:56.366
Let's hear how it worked out
for another pair.

00:33:56.390 --> 00:33:58.200
Alex: Alex Massey. I'm a journalist.

00:33:58.224 --> 00:34:00.726
Jeff: I'm Jeff Harris,
I'm a photographer.